    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "Cursor says a stolen Fireworks AI key led to malicious Claude Code extensions reaching up to 5,000 developers",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "BleepingComputer has the detail. Cursor says an attacker obtained an access key it had issued to Fireworks AI, a provider it used for model training and inference, and used it to copy data from a read-only storage bucket the two companies shared." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What was in the bucket?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Old coding-agent traces, mostly from 2025, from users who had not turned on Privacy Mode. Some of those traces still had API credentials in them, unredacted, and the attacker used those to reach further into Cursor's systems." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Then on October 29th they used a stolen credential to place malicious copies of Anthropic's Claude Code extension directly on the cache that serves Cursor's marketplace. Those sat there for roughly 11 hours, and Cursor estimates up to 5,000 users may have received them." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The extension installed an attacker-controlled SSH key and a way to persist. Cursor says fewer than 0.05% of users had traces exposed and fewer than 0.01% had credentials compromised — but those are Cursor's own figures, and they are not independently verified." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Researchers also found coding errors that likely stopped the backdoor working on many Windows and macOS machines where SSH wasn't already enabled. BleepingComputer places this in a wider wave of attacks on AI developer platforms that Wiz has called Black October." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "US seizes infrastructure behind North Korean 'FaceStealer' campaign that stole more than $400 million in cryptocurrency",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Staying with BleepingComputer, and this one is an update — the vendor research behind it was published before our window opened." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The US government has seized the infrastructure behind a North Korean social engineering campaign called FaceStealer. BleepingComputer says it stole more than $400 million in cryptocurrency, ran for 18 months, and that authorities identified more than 900 webcam videos the hackers had secretly recorded of tech workers." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Recorded to use on whom?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "On the next victim. Per the FBI alert, the attackers hijacked accounts on Telegram, LinkedIn, Slack and Discord, invited targets to a video meeting, then played back footage of people the target knew who had already been caught, before delivering a malicious fix." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Where does the AI come in?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Arctic Wolf Labs flagged 349 videos in the actor's media library as likely modified, as of November 6th, and names Google Generative AI, Grok Imagine, Wondershare AIGC and GPT-4o as tools in the editing workflow. It counted 677 targets across 58 countries, up from more than 400 across 20 countries in April." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And Arctic Wolf is careful about what those counts mean. They describe who the actor prepared material against, not confirmed compromises, and the vendor's figures are not independently verified." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "That's the security file. Now to the chips, and an export-control story that keeps getting wider." }
      ]
