      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "Cursor says a stolen Fireworks AI key led to malicious Claude Code extensions reaching up to 5,000 developers",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "BleepingComputer has the detail. Cursor says an attacker obtained an access key it had issued to Fireworks AI, a provider it used for model training and inference, and copied data from a read-only storage bucket Cursor shared with Fireworks." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What was in the bucket?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Old coding-agent traces, mostly from 2025, from users who had not turned on Privacy Mode. Some of those traces still had API credentials in them, unredacted, and the attacker used those to reach further into Cursor's systems." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Then on October 29th they used a stolen credential to place malicious copies of Anthropic's Claude Code extension directly on the cache that serves Cursor's marketplace. Those sat there for roughly 11 hours, and Cursor estimates up to 5,000 users who requested the affected versions may have received the malicious builds." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The extension installed an attacker-controlled SSH key and a way to persist. Cursor says fewer than 0.05% of users had coding-agent traces exposed and fewer than 0.01% of customers had API credentials compromised — but those are Cursor's own figures, and they are not independently verified." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Researchers also found coding errors that likely stopped the backdoor working on many Windows and macOS machines where SSH wasn't already enabled." }
      ]
    },
